    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old female undergoes a total hip arthroplasty via a standard posterior approach. Postoperatively, the patient is at highest risk for dislocation if the hip is placed in which of the following positions?', '["Flexion, adduction, and internal rotation", "Flexion, adduction, and external rotation", "Flexion, abduction, and external rotation", "Extension and internal rotation", "Extension, abduction, and internal rotation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In a posterior approach to the hip, the typical position of instability causing dislocation is flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. The Sciatic nerve is also at risk during this approach. Patients are routinely placed on specific hip precautions to avoid these extreme ranges of motion early in the postoperative period.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old sustains a fall and suffers a periprosthetic femur fracture around their total hip arthroplasty. Radiographs show the fracture is Around the stem, stem is well-fixed. This is classified as Vancouver B1. What is the most appropriate management?', '["ORIF with intramedullary nailing", "ORIF with plates and cables", "Revision arthroplasty with a longer stem", "Nonoperative management with protected weight-bearing", "Revision arthroplasty with proximal femoral replacement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Vancouver classification dictates treatment of postoperative periprosthetic femur fractures. Vancouver B1 fractures occur Around the stem, stem is well-fixed and are best managed by ORIF with plates and cables.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old sustains a fall and suffers a periprosthetic femur fracture around their total hip arthroplasty. Radiographs show the fracture is Well below the stem tip. This is classified as Vancouver C. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Revision arthroplasty with a longer stem", "ORIF with plate fixation", "Revision arthroplasty with proximal femoral replacement", "Nonoperative management with protected weight-bearing", "ORIF with plates and cables"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Vancouver classification dictates treatment of postoperative periprosthetic femur fractures. Vancouver C fractures occur Well below the stem tip and are best managed by ORIF with plate fixation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old sustains a fall and suffers a periprosthetic femur fracture around their total hip arthroplasty. Radiographs show the fracture is Around the stem, stem is loose. This is classified as Vancouver B2. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Revision arthroplasty with a longer stem", "ORIF with plates and cables", "Revision arthroplasty with proximal femoral replacement", "Nonoperative management with protected weight-bearing", "ORIF with intramedullary nailing"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Vancouver classification dictates treatment of postoperative periprosthetic femur fractures. Vancouver B2 fractures occur Around the stem, stem is loose and are best managed by Revision arthroplasty with a longer stem.'),
