    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old patient with end-stage post-traumatic ankle osteoarthritis is seeking surgical intervention. Which of the following patient profiles is considered the MOST ideal candidate for a total ankle arthroplasty (TAA) rather than an ankle arthrodesis?', '["An older, lower-demand patient with relatively good bone stock and normal hindfoot alignment", "A patient with marked, uncorrectable varus deformity of the hindfoot", "A patient with a history of deep soft-tissue infection in the affected ankle", "A patient with severe avascular necrosis of the talus", "A young, high-demand laborer with a physically strenuous job"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Total ankle arthroplasty is generally preferred for older, lower-demand individuals who wish to preserve range of motion. Contraindications typically include high physical demand, severe malalignment, prior infection, and poor bone stock (e.g., extensive AVN of the talus). Arthodesis remains the gold standard for high-demand patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old male undergoes a total hip arthroplasty via a standard posterior approach. Postoperatively, the patient is at highest risk for dislocation if the hip is placed in which of the following positions?', '["Extension and internal rotation", "Flexion, abduction, and external rotation", "Extension, abduction, and internal rotation", "Flexion, adduction, and internal rotation", "Flexion, adduction, and external rotation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In a posterior approach to the hip, the typical position of instability causing dislocation is flexion, adduction, and internal rotation. The Sciatic nerve is also at risk during this approach. Patients are routinely placed on specific hip precautions to avoid these extreme ranges of motion early in the postoperative period.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old patient presents with increasing pain in the hip 3 weeks after a total hip arthroplasty. Aspiration yields purulent fluid. What is the most likely responsible organism and the standard surgical management?', '["Streptococcus pyogenes; Suppressive intravenous antibiotics only", "Staphylococcus epidermidis; Debridement, antibiotics, and implant retention (DAIR)", "Cutibacterium acnes; One-stage exchange arthroplasty", "Staphylococcus aureus; Debridement, antibiotics, and implant retention (DAIR)", "Staphylococcus aureus; Two-stage exchange arthroplasty"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Periprosthetic joint infections present differently based on timing. Acute infections (typically < 3-4 weeks) are often caused by highly virulent organisms like Staphylococcus aureus and can sometimes be managed with Debridement, antibiotics, and implant retention (DAIR). Chronic infections are usually due to less virulent organisms like Staph epidermidis and require a two-stage exchange.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old patient presents with a painful left hip and a palpable soft-tissue mass. They underwent a metal-on-metal total hip arthroplasty 10 years ago. Serum cobalt and chromium levels are elevated. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Aseptic loosening due to polyethylene wear", "Heterotopic ossification", "Adverse local tissue reaction (ALTR) / Pseudotumor", "Periprosthetic joint infection", "Iliopsoas bursitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Metal-on-metal hip implants can produce metal ion wear debris, which can trigger an adverse local tissue reaction (ALTR), often manifesting as a solid or cystic pseudotumor, pain, and elevated serum metal ions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old patient presents with a painful left hip and a palpable soft-tissue mass. They underwent a metal-on-metal total hip arthroplasty 10 years ago. Serum cobalt and chromium levels are elevated. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Aseptic loosening due to polyethylene wear", "Heterotopic ossification", "Periprosthetic joint infection", "Adverse local tissue reaction (ALTR) / Pseudotumor", "Iliopsoas bursitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Metal-on-metal hip implants can produce metal ion wear debris, which can trigger an adverse local tissue reaction (ALTR), often manifesting as a solid or cystic pseudotumor, pain, and elevated serum metal ions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old patient is undergoing a total joint arthroplasty. The surgical team is reviewing the risk of perioperative systemic complications. Regarding wound dehiscence, which of the following is most accurate in this setting?', '["It is solely caused by the type of implant bearing surface used", "It is completely preventable with prophylactic antibiotics", "Associated with early excessive flexion or poor nutrition", "It only occurs after the 3-month postoperative mark", "It is a rare complication seen almost exclusively in young patients"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Wound dehiscence is a known systemic complication following major joint arthroplasty. Specifically, it is associated with early excessive flexion or poor nutrition. Proper medical optimization and perioperative care are crucial for risk mitigation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old patient presents with vague, progressive shoulder pain 1 year after an anatomic total shoulder arthroplasty. Inflammatory markers are minimally elevated, and there is no frank purulence on aspiration. Intraoperative cultures eventually grow a slow-growing anaerobic Gram-positive bacillus. Which organism is most likely responsible?', '["Staphylococcus aureus", "Streptococcus pyogenes", "Staphylococcus epidermidis", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Cutibacterium acnes"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Cutibacterium (formerly Propionibacterium) acnes is a common skin flora around the shoulder and a frequent cause of indolent, chronic periprosthetic joint infections following shoulder arthroplasty. It often requires prolonged incubation (up to 14 days) to grow in culture.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old patient presents 6 months after a posterior-stabilized total knee arthroplasty complaining of a painful catch or ''clunk'' when extending the knee from a flexed position. What is the most likely etiology of this symptom?', '["Polyethylene wear of the patellar button", "Infection with Staphylococcus epidermidis", "Aseptic loosening of the tibial component", "Rupture of the patellar tendon", "Fibrous nodule formation at the superior pole of the patella"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Patellar clunk syndrome occurs in some posterior-stabilized TKAs when a fibrous nodule forms at the superior pole of the patella. As the knee extends, this nodule catches in the intercondylar box of the femoral component and then pops out with a painful ''''clunk''''.');
